[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it is three
problems with three different fixes. The month-first parser fix will not close the other two.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one bug. Shipping the parser fix and closing #4471 leaves two live problems
with no ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Counts come from the export at render time: one row per report id (the export pages overlap),
placed by tenant and form with `causes.ts`, and the desk's one-day list (`desk-notes.json`)
taken out as its own problem.

## The three problems

**Parser (`parser`).** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file
writes the day first, so any date whose day is 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped.
Reports say "7 April went out on 4 July". This is the fix #4471 has queued.

**booking-v3 date hint (`hint`).** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, but
the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint tells them and
get the swap. It only shows on the `form` channel at tenants that run booking-v3 (Alder Physio,
Copper Studio, Pale Harbour). The parser fix does not touch it: the hint or the form has to change.

**One-day move (`one-day`).** Five reports are not a swap at all. The date moves by one day:
"12 May" shows as "11 May", "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday", one meeting reached a client
in Lisbon as Tuesday and a client in Toronto as Monday, and "every booking in June is one day
off". That points at time zones (an invite sent as local midnight read in another zone), not at
date order. The desk already listed these in `desk-notes.json`; the old table only footnoted them.

## Why they ended up together

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and every
  date report has a subject like "Wrong day on the invite". It accepted this risk; nobody split
  the ticket later.
- Intake tags each report from the reporter's **browser** locale, not the tenant. An `en-US`
  browser at a day-first tenant gets tagged `hint`, a day-first browser on a booking-v3 form
  gets tagged `parser`. The "intake tag wrong" column counts those.
- [Triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) has not been re-read since 2024 and is marked stale.

## What the desk does now

1. Keep #4471 for the parser problem only.
2. Track the hint and one-day problems on their own tickets (linked in the table).
3. New date reports: place them by tenant and channel (`causeOf`), not by subject, and read the
   body for a one-day move before merging.

